WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:07.170
ABC News: From coast to coast in every corner
of the U.S. deaths from fentanyl overdoses

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.170
are soaring.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:13.679
CBS News: The fentanyl epidemic in the United
States killed more than 72,000...72,000 people

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:14.679
in 2022.

00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:18.180
USA Today: Most people who take fentanyl don’t
even know they’re taking it.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.020
You can’t see it, taste it, or smell it.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:26.050
Associated Press: And we know that this global
fentanyl supply chain which ends with the

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:36.160
deaths of Americans often starts with chemical
companies in China.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:46.379
Gabrielle SIERRA: More than 100,000 Americans
died of a drug overdose last year, and at

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.900
the forefront of this crisis is fentanyl.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:54.719
The illicit drug has been a leading killer
of young Americans for the last decade, and

00:00:54.719 --> 00:01:00.360
while the U.S. has faced drug epidemics before,
what we are now encountering is truly unprecedented.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:06.270
SIERRA: While fentanyl addiction and overdose
is a domestic challenge, it is also an international

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:07.270
one.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:11.659
U.S. officials are looking abroad to China
and Mexico in an attempt to curb abuse at

00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:12.659
home.

00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:16.950
China, because it’s the leading supplier
of fentanyl precursors - the chemicals used

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:21.990
to create the drug - and Mexico, because it
is where much of that final assembly happens

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.020
before the drug is smuggled into the United
States.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.340
SIERRA: Both countries are important in this
conversation, but today, we’re going to

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.659
focus on China.

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:36.040
Particularly because the current state of
U.S.-China tensions has strained cooperation

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.950
in a number of areas.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.740
Controlling illicit drug flows looks to be
one of them.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.310
SIERRA: I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is
Why It Matters.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:51.610
Today, China’s role in the U.S. fentanyl
crisis.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:02:00.340
SIERRA: Can you describe for us what fentanyl
is and why it’s so addictive?

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.960
Thomas J. BOLLYKY: So fentanyl is a synthetic
opioid.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:11.940
It can produce an intense high, even with
small amounts, feelings of euphoria and happiness

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.680
on the individual who's taking it.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.510
SIERRA: This is Tom Bollyky.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:20.120
He’s the director of the global health program
here at the Council.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:25.400
He is also the founder and managing editor
of Think Global Health, and he previously

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.420
worked in the office of the U.S. Trade Representative.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:33.849
BOLLYKY: If you take too much, it can lead
to your body effectively shutting down.

00:02:33.849 --> 00:02:40.090
And that is what happens to people who have
taken too much, which is in the fentanyl case,

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.610
not hard to do given its potency.

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:46.000
It is not that expensive to make.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.159
And it can be made from relatively common
precursors, which makes it really a perfect

00:02:52.159 --> 00:02:59.250
product for smuggling into this country, in
that small amounts can be used to boost the

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.099
effect of narcotics.

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:02.459
It can be easily hidden.

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:06.319
It can be easily made and sold for a tremendous
profit.

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:10.420
SIERRA: Fentanyl is actually an FDA approved
drug.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:15.000
It was first introduced in the 1960s as a
powerful intravenous painkiller.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.640
Currently, pharmaceutical fentanyl can be
used before surgery, prescribed by doctors

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:25.170
to treat severe pain after surgery or during
advanced stages of terminal illnesses.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:30.450
SIERRA: So while the purest forms of fentanyl
have these legitimate medical applications,

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:36.069
fentanyl analogues - which are the synthetic
combinations of fentanyl and other drugs - are

00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:38.840
primarily what appears on the black market.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:44.670
SIERRA: And while pure fentanyl is approximately
60 times stronger than morphine, fentanyl

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:50.610
analogues can be anywhere from 600 to 10,000
times more powerful.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:58.110
BOLLYKY: It's paired often with illegal prescription
drugs, heroin typically.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:05.810
But it has slowly now made its way into being
combined or cut into other drugs like methamphetamines

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:06.810
and cocaine.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:15.640
So as it became harder to abuse legal prescription
drugs, people began to shift to illicit prescription

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.760
drugs.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:25.449
And it is in that shift, really around 2012,
that you started to see the rise or the increased

00:04:25.449 --> 00:04:32.610
use of fentanyl, which now plays a major role
in most opioid overdoses in this country.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:37.960
ABC News: Sophia had taken a Percocet not
knowing it was laced with fentanyl...Where

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:43.700
do you think you’d be now?...I think I could
have been in a worse spiral or, as far as

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:48.750
I know, I could have been dead...Bringing
awareness to this issue that that pill may

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:56.060
not be Percocet, that pill may not be Xanax,
that pill may contain fentanyl and potentially

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.820
could be deadly is critical.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.669
BOLLYKY: It's an enormous problem.

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:08.880
Since 1999, drug overdoses in the United States
have killed over a million Americans.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:16.479
For individuals under the age of 40, it is
a leading cause of death in this country.

00:05:16.479 --> 00:05:22.500
It has been bad for some time, but it worsened
dramatically during the pandemic.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:27.570
So to just go through the fatalities, which
paint only a limited picture of what this

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:35.830
has looked like, we went from, in 2019 just
70,000 people in this country dying from drug

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:37.580
overdoses, far too many.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:41.240
That number jumped in 2020 to 93,000.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:42.979
That's a third increase.

00:05:42.979 --> 00:05:52.990
It jumped again in 2021 to 106,000, and then
it climbed marginally again last year to 108,000.

00:05:52.990 --> 00:06:00.280
SIERRA: While that data is for all deaths
due to drug overdose, synthetic opioids including

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:03.349
fentanyl make up the lion’s share.

00:06:03.349 --> 00:06:09.550
Opioid overdoses also inflict roughly $1 trillion
in economic damage every year.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:14.480
SIERRA: Unfortunately, opioid treatment is
not moving fast enough to keep up with the

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:15.840
deadly demand.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:22.310
Federal researchers found that in 2021, of
the 2.5 million people in the U.S. with an

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:27.150
opioid use disorder, only 20 percent received
medication treatment.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:32.990
On top of that, the SUPPORT Act that invested
$20 billion into opioid treatment and prevention

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:40.500
in 2018, just expired last month - and Congress
is not making moves to reup the bill anytime

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.280
soon.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:49.960
SIERRA: Can you walk me through the illicit
fentanyl supply chain?

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.430
Because the drug is clearly getting here somehow
and it seems very accessible.

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:55.490
BOLLYKY: Yeah.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:07:02.330
So the dominant fentanyl supply chain into
this country starts with chemical manufacturers

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:03.699
in China.

00:07:03.699 --> 00:07:10.629
The part of China that's involved with fentanyl
production or the precursors to fentanyl production

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:18.629
are really small businesses, small chemical
companies that are historically been under-regulated.

00:07:18.629 --> 00:07:25.630
After the fentanyl is produced in China, either
in finished form or its precursors, it typically

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:32.590
is shipped to Mexico, where there are two
cartels that dominate the fentanyl trade.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:38.700
It is packaged there either on its own or
cut into other drugs, which then make their

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:45.270
way into the U.S. via the same supply chains
largely that dominate the illicit drug trade

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:47.710
or smuggling into the U.S. generally.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:53.310
What makes this so hard is that pure fentanyl,
just such a small amount can make a big difference,

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:54.620
that potency.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:59.460
There is a recent estimate by a health commission,
the commonwealth commission that looked at

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:05.780
the scale of the U.S. opioid crisis, and they
estimated that just three to five metric tons

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:13.120
of pure fentanyl is needed to supply the entire
U.S. consumption of illegally supplied opioids

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:14.280
in a year.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:19.750
So that is a relatively small amount and much
easier for them to smuggle, particularly when

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:21.450
it is cheaply made.

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:27.520
SIERRA: Historically, the U.S. has stayed
hyper-attentive to illicit drug trade happening

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.580
at our southernmost border, working with Mexican
officials to dismantle numerous pill pressing

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.919
and smuggling operations.

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:41.459
More than 25,000 pounds of fentanyl has been
seized at the U.S.-Mexico border so far this

00:08:41.459 --> 00:08:42.459
year.

00:08:42.459 --> 00:08:48.750
To put this into perspective, just 100 pounds
of fentanyl is lethal enough to kill 18 million

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:53.930
people, which is equivalent to the entire
population of New Jersey and New York City

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:54.930
combined.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:09:00.140
SIERRA: Earlier this month the pressure yielded
a firm result: a powerful faction within the

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:05.620
Sinaloa Cartel, which the U.S. considers the
top fentanyl trafficking group, reportedly

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:08.810
banned their members from producing and selling
the drug.

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:13.500
SIERRA: Policy makers in the U.S. are now
calling for a counter-narcotic strategy that

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:19.690
cuts illicit fentanyl manufacturing off at
the source, which means relying on the cooperation

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:21.000
of China.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:26.089
SIERRA: Could you walk me through China’s
role in all of this?

00:09:26.089 --> 00:09:31.300
Zongyuan Zoe LIU: I would characterize China's
role in the fentanyl market, into two periods,

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:37.030
before May 2019 and after May 2019.

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:38.959
SIERRA: This is Zoe Liu.

00:09:38.959 --> 00:09:41.779
She’s a fellow for China studies at the
Council.

00:09:41.779 --> 00:09:50.360
LIU: And the reason I make this distinction
is because effective on May 1, 2019, China

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:56.980
banned fentanyl as a whole class in terms
of production or fentanyl precursor chemicals,

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:59.140
at least in the domestic market.

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:07.500
And before May 2019, at least, information
and data from U.S. Drug Enforcement Agency

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:13.520
as well as Department of Justice and other
places shows that China is the world leading

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.230
fentanyl export in particular to the United
States.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:23.180
But the Chinese government always cites stories
saying that, “Well, there is no official

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:28.690
data or any indications saying that China
is exporting fentanyl to the United States.”

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:30.880
So, you see the tension there.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:36.740
And the chemical component of fentanyl just
make it so easy to have additional derivatives.

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:42.600
So, given that China already produces a lot
of chemicals as well as a lot of money going

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:44.610
into the Chinese pharmaceutical market.

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:50.230
And knowing that a very important industrial
policy component under the Xi Jinping regime

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:55.149
is prioritizing life science and pharmaceuticals.

00:10:55.149 --> 00:11:02.459
Yes, China is not the go-to type of a high
tech or most innovative area for drug technology

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:07.050
breakthroughs, but it is actually the second
largest pharmaceutical market in the world,

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:08.710
second only to the United States.

00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:16.060
And some reports estimate that by 2025, the
market size of the pharmaceuticals industry

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:18.930
in China is going to be higher than $300 billion.

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:20.520
SIERRA: Oh wow.

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:26.000
And overall, obviously, our relationship with
China in terms of trade is huge.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:27.000
LIU: Yes.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:32.950
U.S.-China trade relationship is perhaps the
single most important bilateral trade relationship,

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:34.050
if you will, in the world.

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:39.710
And if we have to put a price tag on it as
of last year it's about $700 billion.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:41.730
SIERRA: Woah.

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:46.470
SIERRA: And a big portion of this price tag
is pharmaceuticals.

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:51.990
Last year, the United States imported about
$10 billion in pharmaceutical products from

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:52.990
China.

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:58.279
That made up around 10 percent of the $100
billion in total revenue generated by China’s

00:11:58.279 --> 00:12:00.420
powerful pharmaceutical sector.

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:05.699
LIU: Because China is already the second largest
market for pharmaceuticals, there are so many

00:12:05.699 --> 00:12:10.589
- more than 5,000 of different varieties of
small pharmaceutical companies that are making

00:12:10.589 --> 00:12:12.740
different types of chemicals.

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:17.730
So, the entire supply chain becomes individual
makers.

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:25.800
SIERRA: How does illegal fentanyl or fentanyl-related
precursors get trafficked out of China?

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:34.350
LIU: Inside of China, they can either directly
export or traffic through Hong Kong or Yunnan.

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:41.800
Yunnan is a border town between China and
Southeast Asia, and the capital city is Kunming,

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:49.040
which has for a long period of time, has been,
preferred, if you will, drug trafficking route

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:50.550
and so is Hong Kong.

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:57.390
So, conventionally heroin or cocaine, go through
Hong Kong or Yunnan and then through Southeast

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:59.800
Asia, the whole idea of the golden triangle.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:05.480
And obviously, things produced in Afghanistan
or Southeast Asia like Myanmar or Vietnam

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:09.350
or Thailand could also go through this route
to flow into China.

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:14.100
And the Chinese government cracked down on
the drug inflow into the Chinese market, but

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:18.620
they are not necessarily paying the same amount
of attention to export.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:23.860
BOLLYKY: China has a robust chemical manufacturing
industry.

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:30.080
Actually when I was in government, we spent
a lot of time working with trying to improve

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:36.620
the oversight of that chemical industry, which
was at the time, tied for substituting dangerous

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:42.800
products like diethylene glycol into products
called excipients, things you add to small

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:46.320
molecule pharmaceuticals to make them easier
to take.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:52.399
And at the time, China was not regulating
its chemical industry at all if the products

00:13:52.399 --> 00:13:54.630
were destined for export.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:14:04.070
China has shown little historical appetite
in overseeing the export of products from

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:05.579
that industry.

00:14:05.579 --> 00:14:11.449
The only way we made progress in the import
safety crisis is that it had risen to such

00:14:11.449 --> 00:14:17.320
a scale and a high level of poisoning, and
these were regulated products by the FDA,

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:24.070
that China was legitimately worried about
its brand, its ability to export into the

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:25.070
United States.

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:30.839
Or for other partners, using Chinese products
in the supply chain to be able to export to

00:14:30.839 --> 00:14:36.870
the United States, that they felt compelled
to enter into a memorandum of understanding

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:39.050
to do something about this industry.

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:44.590
SIERRA: In recent years, the Biden administration
has begun to crack down on fentanyl suppliers,

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:47.149
developing stronger counter-narcotic strategies.

00:14:47.149 --> 00:14:52.779
Actually, our own Latin America studies fellow
Shannon O’Neil gave testimony before Congress

00:14:52.779 --> 00:14:57.820
about the enormous human toll of the illicit
drug trade, and suggested partnerships with

00:14:57.820 --> 00:15:02.920
our Western counterparts in Latin America
to reduce the supply of illicit drugs.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:07.010
You can check it out in the show notes.

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:14.190
LIU: For the very first time this year in
June, our American Department of Justice indicted

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:22.640
four Chinese companies and eight Chinese nationals
for crimes related to making or trafficking

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:26.320
or the sales of fentanyl and fentanyl precursors.

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:33.670
Now, these four companies are based in China,
and the Chinese government reaction to the

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:39.370
Department of Justice indictment is that is
there is no base of it.

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:45.310
Because the profitability is so high, not
just criminal organizations in Mexico are

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:46.899
interested in it.

00:15:46.899 --> 00:15:52.070
Actually, normal Chinese companies, if there
is opportunity for them, they are interested

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:54.110
in doing it.

00:15:54.110 --> 00:15:59.920
SIERRA: But the U.S. has this unique problem,
and China somehow does not, correct?

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:00.920
BOLLYKY: That's correct.

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:07.150
So China has no incentive on its own to do
anything on fentanyl.

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:10.079
They view it as a U.S. problem.

00:16:10.079 --> 00:16:19.300
And they will very vociferously contest representations
that this is a matter of China pushing products

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.009
onto the U.S.

00:16:21.009 --> 00:16:26.069
Beijing's foreign ministry says no illegal
fentanyl trafficking happens between China

00:16:26.069 --> 00:16:27.069
and Mexico.

00:16:27.069 --> 00:16:30.089
Adding ‘it’s a made in the USA problem’.

00:16:30.089 --> 00:16:35.990
It also criticized Washington for creating
obstacles in their anti-narcotics cooperation

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:38.769
by imposing sanctions on the country.

00:16:38.769 --> 00:16:46.009
BOLLYKY: China's incentive for doing anything
on this product or around this class of drugs,

00:16:46.009 --> 00:16:52.199
is really linked to their motivation to cooperate
with the U.S.more generally.

00:16:52.199 --> 00:16:58.490
And that is tied to China's sets of requests
on areas that they would like to see progress

00:16:58.490 --> 00:16:59.490
on.

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:04.620
And that's really where the relationship or
the cooperation with China in recent years

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:05.910
has broken down.

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:08.579
SIERRA: How about the rest of the world?

00:17:08.579 --> 00:17:10.520
Are we alone in this crisis?

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:13.939
BOLLYKY: We are alone in having a problem
of this scale.

00:17:13.939 --> 00:17:20.709
Opioid use, or synthetic opioid use I should
say has started to increase a bit in other

00:17:20.709 --> 00:17:25.810
countries, but nothing close to the scale
of what we see in the United States.

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:33.299
And that has been one contributing factor
to why the geopolitics of addressing the supply

00:17:33.299 --> 00:17:39.210
of these drugs and supply of their precursors
has been so hard, is that the U.S. is somewhat

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:43.510
anomalous in the scale of the problem.

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:48.120
The U.S. has started in more recent years,
or this year really in particular, to take

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:56.580
a twofold strategy of trying to get this back
on track with China to disrupt production.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:18:04.130
One strategy has been to announce a global
coalition to address synthetic drugs that

00:18:04.130 --> 00:18:05.809
the State Department is leading.

00:18:05.809 --> 00:18:09.960
Secretary Blinken, Anthony Blinken announced,
that would have more than 80 countries involved

00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:18.770
in a time bound effort to try to strengthen
the policies, the regulatory oversight around

00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:19.770
synthetic drugs.

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:23.590
U.S. Department of State: We launched the
Global Coalition to Address Synthetic Drug

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:30.530
Threats in recognition of the scale of this
challenge, and of the need for strong coordinated

00:18:30.530 --> 00:18:31.530
international action.

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:37.670
In July we convened the first ministerial,
joined by officials from more than 80 other

00:18:37.670 --> 00:18:41.980
countries, and leaders from over a dozen international
and regional organizations.

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:47.500
BOLLYKY: This is an effort to try to shift
what has largely been a bilateral conversation

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:52.600
between the U.S. and China about how to do
something on these products, to try to create

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:59.250
some amount of consensus, some reputational
incentive, by bringing together other partners.

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:04.419
Can we multilateralize this, bring in more
countries, create a consensus around it?

00:19:04.419 --> 00:19:06.530
So that's one part of the strategy.

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:12.360
The other part of the strategy is the U.S.
has started prosecuting or announcing indictments

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:19.360
of those small chemical companies or individuals
that are involved in the production of finished

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:24.220
fentanyl or fentanyl precursors for shipment
abroad.

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:29.750
So it is really trying to use both a bit of
a carrot and a stick here.

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:35.380
A carrot being more of a global coalition
to draw China into these discussions, try

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:37.500
to move it from a bilateral discussion.

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:41.120
And the stick of actually going after individuals
and companies.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:45.940
SIERRA: Earlier this month, the U.S. Department
of Justice announced more charges against

00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:50.620
eight companies and twelve of their executives
in connection with fentanyl distribution.

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:54.799
Here’s what Attorney General Merrick Garland
said at a press conference:

00:19:54.799 --> 00:20:00.510
Forbes Breaking News: These companies advertise
the sale of precursor chemicals online using

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:03.100
different websites and social media platforms.

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:08.679
They then ship the building blocks needed
to create deadly drugs all over the world.

00:20:08.679 --> 00:20:13.990
SIERRA: China hasn’t commented on the most
recent indictments, but after the U.S. unveiled

00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:19.190
similar charges in June, China said these
accusations could actually undermine future

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:20.930
cooperation on fentanyl.

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:25.770
Shanghai Eye: The U.S. side should resolve
the issue in an equal, respectful, and cooperative

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:31.179
manner and lift sanctions on China’s anti-drug
agencies as soon as possible, so as to remove

00:20:31.179 --> 00:20:35.700
obstacles to bilateral dialogue and cooperation.

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:44.230
BOLLYKY: I think China, when it is motivated
to oversee its chemical industry, has proven

00:20:44.230 --> 00:20:46.909
itself capable of doing so.

00:20:46.909 --> 00:20:48.559
It's a large industry.

00:20:48.559 --> 00:20:53.670
This requires reaching down to lower levels
of enforcement.

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:59.390
We're talking about something rising up to
the Beijing level, that ordinarily is dealt

00:20:59.390 --> 00:21:04.280
with more and on the provincial or local level
in terms of oversight of these industries.

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:08.309
You need that high level of attention for
progress to be made here.

00:21:08.309 --> 00:21:15.120
But I am confident that when that attention
is given, China is very capable of action

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:16.120
in this space.

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:21.429
They just aren't presently motivated to do
so, and the geopolitics make it seem hard

00:21:21.429 --> 00:21:25.460
to imagine in the short term how that's going
to shift.

00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:29.740
The U.S. has definitely asked China on many
occasions.

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:33.860
And again, their argument is that they adopted
these regulations.

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:41.610
They dispute the proof that China's industry
is a dominant source of fentanyl imported

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:43.679
into the United States.

00:21:43.679 --> 00:21:50.710
They really argue that this is a U.S. problem,
a blight on U.S. society that the U.S. is

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:56.070
trying to blame China for, because it shifts
responsibility elsewhere.

00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:57.490
SIERRA: What do you think?

00:21:57.490 --> 00:22:01.909
I mean, do you think it's fair to point the
finger externally when it's a problem we haven't

00:22:01.909 --> 00:22:02.950
fixed domestically?

00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:10.179
BOLLYKY: I think it's fair to point out that
China is contributing to a U.S. problem.

00:22:10.179 --> 00:22:16.620
It is our problem ultimately, but it is one
that they are making worse, and they're contributing

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:22.400
to, and could act in a manner to mitigate
or reduce.

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:24.549
And they should be expected to do so.

00:22:24.549 --> 00:22:31.380
China has of course, historically had its
own struggles with substance abuse in other

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:32.380
settings.

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:39.049
And in those contexts, was quite sensitive
to the role of other governments in that challenge

00:22:39.049 --> 00:22:40.150
that it had.

00:22:40.150 --> 00:22:45.110
LIU: This is not the first time that the world
is facing a drug abuse crisis.

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:50.890
And for that matter, China would cite the
West of exporting opium to China as early

00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:52.510
as in the 19th century.

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:59.930
And it was also because of the first opium
war that China started to fall into these

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:01.750
hundred years of humiliation.

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:06.820
So, from that perspective, this is not the
first time that we are dealing with drug abuse

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:08.840
or substance abuse problem.

00:23:08.840 --> 00:23:14.080
SIERRA: In the nineteenth and early twentieth
century, China was the target of foreign opium

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:19.950
exports, initially illicit, which spurred
a nationwide addiction crisis.

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:24.929
But after the communist revolution, a major
prohibition began, wiping out most of China’s

00:23:24.929 --> 00:23:26.490
domestic production.

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:31.659
However, after the economic reform of China’s
trade policy in the 1980s, the supply route

00:23:31.659 --> 00:23:34.510
for illicit drug trafficking reopened.

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:41.610
And by 2016, the number of officially registered
drug addicts in China totaled about 2.5 million,

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:47.460
having increased every year since the government’s
first annual drug enforcement report in 1998.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:52.100
SIERRA: In this way, China’s addiction history
is a bit similar to ours.

00:23:52.100 --> 00:23:57.240
The production and sale of OxyContin by Purdue
Pharma, controlled by the Sackler family,

00:23:57.240 --> 00:24:03.420
helped spur a national crisis as opioid-related
deaths skyrocketed in the late-90s and early

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:04.559
2000s.

00:24:04.559 --> 00:24:10.630
Today, fentanyl is hitting a U.S. market already
primed for abuse by the painkiller debacle.

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:15.680
SIERRA: Given China’s own history combating
drug use, their government should be empathetic

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:20.159
with the acute challenges the U.S. is facing
amid this epidemic.

00:24:20.159 --> 00:24:24.920
But so far, they haven’t been too interested
in helping, saying that because China doesn’t

00:24:24.920 --> 00:24:29.799
have a domestic drug issue, they shouldn’t
be expected to help other countries with theirs.

00:24:29.799 --> 00:24:36.210
BOLLYKY: I think it's, from that standpoint,
reasonable to expect them to do their part

00:24:36.210 --> 00:24:37.960
to address this challenge.

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:44.990
But fundamentally, it will not be solved unless
U.S. action is taken, more support is given

00:24:44.990 --> 00:24:49.730
for treatment, more support is given to reduce
the demand for these products.

00:24:49.730 --> 00:24:54.840
LIU: I think it's very easy for us, at least
for the American audience to think that the

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:58.570
Chinese government, even if the Chinese government
does not have an explicit strategy to poison

00:24:58.570 --> 00:25:01.900
American people, they must have been not doing
a good job.

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:03.960
And I think that much is pretty clear.

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:09.750
This becomes this unifying thing that everybody
can bandwagon on and blame it on China.

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:15.200
It becomes this not just bipartisan, but also
unifying at a societal level saying that this

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:16.200
is bad.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:17.370
And who is the largest supplier?

00:25:17.370 --> 00:25:18.830
Who is the largest maker?

00:25:18.830 --> 00:25:19.830
It is China.

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:25.580
The Chinese government indeed hasn't been
doing a good job in terms of enforcing the

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:28.020
substance control, especially with regard
to fentanyl.

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:29.020
Right?

00:25:29.020 --> 00:25:33.549
And especially when you think about comparing
how they deal with heroin and cocaine versus

00:25:33.549 --> 00:25:35.150
the way that it controls fentanyl.

00:25:35.150 --> 00:25:40.080
But I would also want to point out that perhaps
the Chinese government's priority is not there

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:41.080
yet.

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:43.440
They have economic problems, and then security
concerns.

00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:49.830
And China so far does not necessarily have
a fentanyl or opioid crisis, hence, it's not

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:53.169
necessarily up on the government priority
list.

00:25:53.169 --> 00:25:58.809
SIERRA: It is unlikely that China would regulate
fentanyl for benevolent purposes - moving

00:25:58.809 --> 00:26:04.409
it higher on their list of priorities in order
to help the U.S. take care of a domestic issue

00:26:04.409 --> 00:26:09.270
that many feel we alone are responsible for
causing and solving.

00:26:09.270 --> 00:26:15.039
But perhaps this tragic epidemic and China’s
role in supplying fentanyl could open a route

00:26:15.039 --> 00:26:16.380
for partnership opportunities.

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:21.659
SIERRA: On the flip side, do you think that
the fentanyl issue could become an opportunity

00:26:21.659 --> 00:26:26.429
for cooperation in what is otherwise becoming
a chilly relationship, that it could lead

00:26:26.429 --> 00:26:29.289
to greater cooperation in perhaps other arenas?

00:26:29.289 --> 00:26:30.289
LIU: Yes.

00:26:30.289 --> 00:26:36.140
I do think this could be at least one area
for the United States and China to start,

00:26:36.140 --> 00:26:41.240
rebuild or repair their relationship, because
fighting against transnational crime is of

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:44.820
the interest of both the United States and
China.

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:49.150
This issue matters for China for two reasons,
at least.

00:26:49.150 --> 00:26:56.929
The first reason is that repairing the relationship
with the United States can start with something

00:26:56.929 --> 00:27:02.240
as important for the United States or as not
so important for China.

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:07.760
So, in other words, this does not necessarily
take too much trouble for the Chinese government

00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:10.919
to do, given that the regulation is already
there.

00:27:10.919 --> 00:27:16.780
Just regulate fentanyl the same way as other
drugs, this could be a very important way

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:19.500
to repair U.S.-China relations.

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:24.420
Especially after several administrations of
the U.S. government have pressured China to

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:25.430
do it.

00:27:25.430 --> 00:27:32.159
And the second reason it matters for China,
is actually at the Chinese household level.

00:27:32.159 --> 00:27:39.480
I'm genuinely concerned that there may be
huge demand for recreational drugs inside

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:43.429
the Chinese market, specifically because of
the massive youth unemployment problem in

00:27:43.429 --> 00:27:44.429
China.

00:27:44.429 --> 00:27:48.440
The Chinese government stopped publishing
or paused updating youth unemployment numbers

00:27:48.440 --> 00:27:50.000
as of last month.

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:57.720
And the latest update suggested that youth
unemployment was as high as about 21 percent.

00:27:57.720 --> 00:28:05.049
So, when you have younger people who are disincentivized
to work and they are having a hard time getting

00:28:05.049 --> 00:28:12.480
a good job that they desire, it's very enticing
and problematic if they seek recreational

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:15.549
drugs as a way of escape.

00:28:15.549 --> 00:28:21.409
And you see that happen in the United States
in other countries, and there is no reason

00:28:21.409 --> 00:28:24.880
to think that this is not going to happen
to China.

00:28:24.880 --> 00:28:28.330
Therefore, it matters for China for domestic
reasons as well.

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:32.380
It would just become too late for the government
to regulate when it becomes a crisis.

00:28:32.380 --> 00:28:34.940
LIU: I try to be an optimist.

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:39.980
But in this case, I'm sorry, I have to say
that I'm relatively pessimistic.

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:47.640
It is very important for international organizations,
for governments around the world to recognize

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:53.900
that this is the issue that everybody will
be challenged with one way or another.

00:28:53.900 --> 00:29:00.559
And it is a good cause for the government
to cooperate in order to prevent the current

00:29:00.559 --> 00:29:04.539
substance abuse crisis and the next one.

00:29:04.539 --> 00:29:10.390
Unfortunately, so far, really, we don't have
effective multinational or international cooperation

00:29:10.390 --> 00:29:12.320
on drug trafficking.

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:17.620
But perhaps at least the United States and
China for the sake of restoring their relationship,

00:29:17.620 --> 00:29:21.049
for the sake of protecting the lives of their
own nationals, the life of their own nationals,

00:29:21.049 --> 00:29:23.970
they are willing to take a step forward.

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:34.230
BOLLYKY: China, I am entirely confident, is
more capable of regulating its small chemical,

00:29:34.230 --> 00:29:41.070
family owned in many cases, chemical companies,
than it is presently doing around the precursors

00:29:41.070 --> 00:29:42.899
or finished fentanyl.

00:29:42.899 --> 00:29:48.169
China is waiting for the right incentives
from the U.S. before it feels compelled to

00:29:48.169 --> 00:29:49.169
act.

00:29:49.169 --> 00:29:55.730
And I think it's fair for Americans to feel
in the midst of an epidemic where it's devastating

00:29:55.730 --> 00:30:02.480
to families and individual lives, that that's
a pretty cold calculation being done on the

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:04.870
part of the Chinese government.

00:30:04.870 --> 00:30:06.920
I think that's fair.

00:30:06.920 --> 00:30:14.360
But fundamentally at the end of the day, people
need to understand that this is indeed a U.S.

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:15.360
problem.

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:22.640
We need to look ourselves in the mirror and
confront that, and be realistic in noting

00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:29.799
that the only way to sustainably reduce the
opioid problem in this country will not come

00:30:29.799 --> 00:30:38.659
from China, it will be coming from demand
side actions to reduce the use of fentanyl,

00:30:38.659 --> 00:30:47.360
to provide greater access to treatment, to
reduce its potential public health consequences.

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:57.780
